{"name":"Hair Ribbon","hex":"#939cc9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hair","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pigmented filament of keratin which grows from a follicle on the skin of humans and other mammals.","The collection or mass of such growths growing from the skin of humans and animals, and forming a covering for any part or the whole body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove the hair from.","To grow hair (where there was a bald spot)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hair"},{"term":"ribbon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, narrow strip of material used for decoration of clothing or the hair or gift wrapping.","An awareness ribbon."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate with ribbon.","To stripe or streak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ribbon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ribbon","blocks":[{"text":"A ribbon is a thin band of material, typically cloth but also plastic or sometimes metal, used primarily as decorative binding and tying. Cloth ribbons are made of natural materials such as silk, cotton, and jute and of synthetic materials, such as polyester, nylon, and polypropylene. Ribbon is used for useful, ornamental, and symbolic purposes. Cultures around the world use ribbon in their hair, around the body, and as ornament on non-human animals, buildings, and packaging. Some popular fabrics used to make ribbons are satin, organza, sheer, silk, velvet, and grosgrain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Scrummy_ribbons_by_Lainey_Powell.jpg/330px-Scrummy_ribbons_by_Lainey_Powell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbon","wikidata":"Q6918773"},{"title":"Hair","blocks":[{"text":"Hair is a protein filament that grows from follicles found in the dermis. Hair is one of the distinguishing characteristics of mammals.\nThe human body, apart from areas of glabrous skin, is covered in follicles which produce thick terminal and fine vellus hair. Most common interest in hair is focused on hair growth, hair types, and hair care, but hair is also an important biomaterial primarily composed of protein, notably alpha-keratin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Gray945.png/330px-Gray945.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hair","wikidata":"Q28472"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Glaube.jpg/500px-Glaube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Glaube.jpg/1280px-Glaube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Glaube.jpg/500px-Glaube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A hair ribbon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlaube.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Ribbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg/500px-Ribbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Ribbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg/1280px-Ribbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Ribbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg/500px-Ribbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ribbons sculpture by Pippa Hale","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibbons_sculpture_by_Pippa_Hale_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/CSIRO_ScienceImage_8115_Human_hair_and_Merino_wool_fibre.jpg/500px-CSI